          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "What is high blood pressure, and how is it different from one high reading?",
          "answer": "High blood pressure, also known as hypertension, is a condition that often has no single identifiable cause and usually doesn't make a person feel unwell. It is often detected through screening. Factors like age, genetics, body weight, diet (high sodium, low potassium), physical inactivity, alcohol intake, poor sleep, and chronic stress can contribute to it.\n\nA single elevated blood pressure reading is not the same as a diagnosis of high blood pressure. Because high blood pressure often doesn't cause symptoms, its diagnosis relies on measurements over time, not just one reading or how a person feels. (Source: General clinical reference, \"Hypertension (high blood pressure)\")\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
